        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 183"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.087,
            0.687,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "13. Two elements, A and B, have the following configurations —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.108,
            0.374,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "A: 2, 8, 5 B: 2, 8, 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.129,
            0.49,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "(i) Which element is more reactive?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.146,
            0.772,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Will A and B form ionic or covalent bonds when they combine? Explain using electron transfer or sharing."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.179,
            0.684,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Predict the formula of the compound they would form."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.2,
            0.772,
            0.235
          ],
          "content": "14. Assertion (A): Copper sulfate conducts electricity in the molten state but not in the solid state."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.237,
            0.772,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": "Reason (R): Copper and sulfate ions are fixed in the lattice in molten state, while in solid state they can move freely."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.275,
            0.39,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": "Choose the correct option:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.295,
            0.725,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "(i) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.313,
            0.753,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.33,
            0.399,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "(iii) A is true, but R is false."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.346,
            0.399,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "(iv) A is false, but R is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.366,
            0.772,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "15. The species 27<sub>Al</sub>, 80<sub>Br</sub>– and 201<sub>Hg</sub><sup>2+</sup> have 13, 35 and 80 protons, respectively. How many electrons and neutrons do they have?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.462,
            0.59,
            0.486
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "The Journey Beyond"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.52,
            0.776,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "y Design and perform an experiment to show and compare that water always contains hydrogen and oxygen in the same ratio, regardless of its source."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.574,
            0.775,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "y Compare atoms and ions of any three elements. Show the number of electrons before and after ion formation using bar graphs."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.611,
            0.775,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": "y Make a card game with cations and anions. Possible ideas may include —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.633,
            0.775,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "Pick cards from the pile or from open cards and match them with cards in your hand to form compounds. You may discard any unwanted cards."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.687,
            0.774,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "Ask other players for cards and form compounds using the cards they already have."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.729,
            0.765,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "y To learn more about molecules you can explore the link given below —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.75,
            0.774,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/build-a-molecule/latest/build-a-molecule\\_all.html"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.807,
            0.399,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": "**The Quest Continues …**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.839,
            0.756,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "Are there any chemical changes that do not obey the Law of Conservation of Mass?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
